`VirtualFileSystemImpl` forwarded every call to `createVirtualFileSystem()` and nothing else:

```ts
/**
 * @deprecated Use createVirtualFileSystem() factory function instead.
 */
export class VirtualFileSystemImpl implements VirtualFileSystem {
  private readonly vfs: VirtualFileSystem

  constructor(enableDebugLogging = false) {
    this.vfs = createVirtualFileSystem(enableDebugLogging)
  }

  getCurrentDirectory(): string {
    return this.vfs.getCurrentDirectory()
  }
  // ... 11 more, all identical
}
```

All 12 methods were one-line passthroughs — verified individually, no added logic anywhere. It was marked deprecated when the factory was introduced, but nothing ever migrated: the worker and every test still constructed the wrapper.

`moo-dang` is a private app with no external consumers, so the compatibility shim had nobody to be compatible with.

## Change

Replaces `new VirtualFileSystemImpl(x)` with `createVirtualFileSystem(x)` at all six call sites — `workers/shell.worker.ts` plus five test files — and deletes the class.

One wrinkle worth noting: the `VirtualFileSystem` interface is declared in `shell/types.ts`, not re-exported from `virtual-file-system.ts`. Typing the call sites against the interface therefore required importing it from `./types`, not from the module the factory lives in.

`virtual-file-system.test.ts`'s `describe` block is renamed from `VirtualFileSystemImpl` to `createVirtualFileSystem`. Every assertion is unchanged — only construction and the block name differ.

`job-controller.test.ts` reaches the module through a dynamic `await import('./virtual-file-system')`, so its destructured binding was updated rather than an import statement.

No `instanceof VirtualFileSystemImpl` existed anywhere, and the class was not re-exported from any barrel, so nothing depended on class identity.

## Verification [description truncated to fit budget]

`runPerformanceRegression` could not detect a performance regression. It reported success unconditionally.

```ts
const hasRegression = false          // never reassigned

await runFullPerformanceSuite(ciOptions)

// TODO: Load baseline and compare results
if (baselineFile) {
  consola.info(`Would compare against baseline: ${baselineFile}`)
  // Implement baseline comparison logic here
}

if (hasRegression) {
  consola.error('Performance regression detected!')
  process.exit(1)
} else {
  consola.success('No performance regression detected')
}
```

The baseline comparison was never written. `hasRegression` is a `const false`, so the `process.exit(1)` branch is unreachable. Passing a baseline file logs `Would compare against ...` and changes nothing — the run still ends in `No performance regression detected`.

It was reachable through `runBenchmarkCLI`'s `case 'regression'`, so wiring `tsx src/test/performance-runner.ts regression <baseline>` into CI would have produced a permanently green gate that had never checked anything. That is worse than having no check, because it looks like coverage.

## Why delete rather than fix

Nothing depends on it. No `package.json` script exposes the `regression` mode, no workflow invokes it, and no documentation promises it — every "regression" reference in the repo turned out to be the Playwright/Storybook visual regression suite, which is unrelated.

A real performance gate needs decisions this stub never made: where baselines are stored, how they are recorded and refreshed, what threshold counts as a regression, and how noisy CI runners are handled. None of that is recoverable from `// Implement baseline comparison logic here`. If the capability is wanted, it should be designed against those questions rather than grown from a stub that has been reporting success since it was written. [description truncated to fit budget]
